{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["And it shall be — if they do not believe this sign alone, for they will say: since there are among them informers (דֵּלָטוֹרִין) like crawling things of the dust, [Israel] was cast down the full height of their stature to the ground; if so, as long as this cause persists, there is no remedy. Therefore it is stated: ״וַיֹּאמֶר ה׳ לוֹ עוֹד הָבֵא נָא יָדְךָ בְּחֵיקֶךָ״. In the word עוֹד He indicated that the first sign, which signified the nullification of the effect, was not sufficient, for every effect cannot be nullified as long as the cause persists; therefore you need a second sign which signifies the nullification of the cause, and nullification of the cause is an indication of the nullification of the effect, for the one depends on the other. And the cause — we have already said that the sin of the tongue brought all this upon them: whether on account of the sin of Abraham's saying ״בַּמָּה אֵדָע״ (Genesis 15:8), whether on account of Joseph's bringing an evil report of them (Genesis 37:2) — for through this the matter rolled onward and they descended to Egypt — whether because there were informers among them, as it is written ״אָכֵן נוֹדַע הַדָּבָר״ (Exodus 2:14). And the Holy One, blessed be He, showed him that the cause was nullified, for from now on there is no more evil tongue among them, and He hinted at the matter through what is stated ״הָבֵא נָא יָדְךָ בְּחֵיקֶךָ וְגוֹ׳ וְהִנֵּה מְצֹרַעַת כַּשָּׁלֶג״. And according to our Rabbis of blessed memory (Exodus Rabbah 3:13), he was stricken with leprosy because he spoke evil tongue against Israel and said ״וְהֵן לֹא יַאֲמִינוּ לִי״ (Exodus 4:1), and this is a sign that there are no longer in Israel men of discord and evil tongue — for had there been among them men of discord and evil tongue, Moses would not have been punished on their account, for did not our Rabbis of blessed memory say (Jerusalem Talmud Peah 1:1) that it is permitted to speak evil tongue against men of discord? Rather, because this affliction had been healed from Israel, therefore Moses was punished on their account — as they said of blessed memory (Leviticus Rabbah 32:5) that Israel were redeemed on account of the fact that there was no evil tongue among them. Also according to its plain sense, it was hinted through the healing of Moses' hand that the sin of the tongue had been healed from Israel — and this nullification of the cause, which is a sign upon the nullification of the effect, namely the Egyptian exile. And further on, in the portion of Korah, on the verse ״כַּאֲשֶׁר דִּבֶּר ה׳ בְּיַד מֹשֶׁה לוֹ״ (Numbers 17:5), another reason for the leprosy of Moses' hand will be explained, with the help of HaShem."], :book :exodus, :voice :kli-yakar, :lang :en, :chapter 4, :notes {0 "The term דֵּלָטוֹרִין is a rabbinic loanword from Latin delator (informer/slanderer); rendered as 'informers' to preserve the communal-betrayal sense central to the argument."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:18:27.150157555Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}